% ============================================================================
% digraph-sound — common consonant digraphs and the single speech sound each
% one represents (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE). A K-2 phonics library, grounding
% ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1's "phonics, decoding" Major Gap with a genuinely
% NEW angle for this loop's `wave2-k8-literacy-foundations` item, via a FRESH
% WebFetch rather than the sibling-table-mining technique this item's own
% notes record as exhausted stdlib-wide.
% ============================================================================
% A consonant DIGRAPH is two consonant letters that together spell ONE speech
% sound — "sh" in "ship" is not "s" plus "h", it is a single /sh/ sound. This
% is categorically distinct from every phonics table already shipped in this
% library: `r-controlled-vowel-word.adj` names a VOWEL-plus-r pattern in whole
% WORDS, `silent-e-word.adj`/`syllable-type-alias.adj` name a SYLLABLE
% structure, and `past-tense-ed-sound.adj`/`plural-s-sound.adj` name how an
% ENDING is pronounced depending on context — none of them tables a plain
% two-letter spelling-to-sound mapping for a word-initial/medial consonant
% digraph, the single most common K-2 phonics unit still missing from this
% library. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? digraph_sound(sh, $Sound)
%       % sh_sound
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `digraph_sound(digraph, sound)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% sound WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% any digraph the cited page's lesson table does not name. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a sound and recall which digraph carries it:
%
%     ? digraph_sound($D, ch_sound)
%       % ch
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `th` has TWO rows: the cited page's lesson table splits
% "th" into two
% separate lessons, one per pronunciation ("th /th/ (voiced)", as in "this",
% and "th /th/ (unvoiced)", as in "think") rather than treating "th" as a
% single sound the way it treats every other digraph here. This table
% reproduces that split faithfully as two rows sharing the same `digraph`
% key rather than collapsing it into one — the SAME one-key/many-values shape
% `opposites.adj`/`synonyms.adj`/`homophones.adj` already established
% elsewhere in this library (there for one word with several antonyms or
% synonyms; here for one spelling with two genuinely different sounds). A
% forward query on `th` therefore yields BOTH rows, not one — an honest
% reflection of the page's lesson table, not an omission.
%
% Provenance — the `source` and the ROWS are not grounded by the same thing,
% and this note says which is which. Both come from the University of
% Florida Literacy Institute (UFLI)'s Foundations Toolbox, "Digraphs Unit
% Resources (Lessons 42-53)" page, curl-fetched directly rather than through
% an AI-summarized WebFetch.
%
% The `source` is that page's opening definition paragraph, quoted WHOLE and
% byte for byte: "A consonant digraph is a combination of two consonant
% letters that represent a single consonant<U+00A0>speech sound.<U+00A0> The
% lessons in this unit are designed to strengthen students’ familiarity
% with<U+00A0>consonant digraphs."
%
% The `<U+00A0>` marks are NOTATION, not text: the page uses NO-BREAK SPACES
% at those 3 positions and the `source` field carries the real character.
% They are written out here because a literal U+00A0 inside a wrapped
% comment is invisible to a reader and a break opportunity to a rewrapper —
% the two ways this byte has already gone missing once.
%
% It states what a consonant digraph IS. It names no individual digraph and
% no sound, so it grounds none of the nine rows. Those come from elsewhere
% on the same page — the unit's lesson table, whose "Concept" column names
% each lesson's digraph with its sound. Those cells extract as SEPARATE
% BLOCKS, one per cell, which is exactly the cross-block/table-row class
% held open on #14111 ("is a table row a verbatim span?"), so they are
% deliberately NOT presented here as a quoted span, and each row below names
% the cell it reads instead.
%
% Until installment 4g the `source` field carried the definition sentences
% with a hand-assembled rendering of that table STITCHED ONTO THEM, a string
% that appears nowhere on the page: it invented a colon after the unit
% heading, invented a separator between each lesson number and the previous
% cell, dropped the paragraph's closing sentence, and flattened the page's
% U+00A0 between "consonant" and "speech" to an ASCII space — the defect
% security review found after the first repair, since the extractor the
% repair read from normalises that byte away. It never reached an
% apostrophe, so unlike three of its four sibling libraries it flattened
% none of those. A composition presented as a quotation is a defect in the FIELD, not
% a position on the held question — removing it takes no position either
% way.
%
% Four lessons in the same unit (42 "FLSZ Spelling Rule", 43 "-all, -oll,
% -ull", 49 "Digraphs Review 1", 53 "Digraphs Review 2") are review or
% spelling-rule lessons, not individual digraph-sound lessons, and are
% deliberately NOT rows here.
%
%     digraph (atom label)   sound (atom label)    Concept cell it reads
%     ----------------------  ---------------------  ---------------------------
%     ck                      k_sound                "ck /k/"
%     sh                      sh_sound               "sh /sh/"
%     th                      th_voiced_sound         "th /th/ (voiced)"
%     th                      th_unvoiced_sound       "th /th/ (unvoiced)"
%     ch                      ch_sound               "ch /ch/"
%     wh                      w_sound                "wh /w/, ph /f/"
%     ph                      f_sound                "wh /w/, ph /f/"
%     ng                      ng_sound               "ng /ŋ/"
%     nk                      nk_sound               "nk /ŋk/"
%
% `trust authoritative` — UFLI is a university literacy research center
% (University of Florida, .edu), the same tier and same source family
% `r-controlled-vowel-word.adj` already established for this stdlib. A
% full-tree grep for `digraph`/`\bsh\b`/`\bch\b`/`\bwh\b` across the whole
% shipped stdlib (the mandatory duplication check before scoping) found zero
% table `row` hits naming any consonant digraph's sound — confirming this is
% the FIRST digraph-sound content anywhere in this stdlib. A digraph not one
% of these — e.g. `qu`, a real digraph the SAME UFLI scope-and-sequence
% covers elsewhere, but not in this page's own lesson table — is deliberately
% NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a sound. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table digraph_sound {
    columns digraph, sound

    row (ck, k_sound)             % lesson 44 cell: "ck /k/"
    row (sh, sh_sound)            % lesson 45 cell: "sh /sh/"
    row (th, th_voiced_sound)     % lesson 46 cell: "th /th/ (voiced)"
    row (th, th_unvoiced_sound)   % lesson 47 cell: "th /th/ (unvoiced)"
    row (ch, ch_sound)            % lesson 48 cell: "ch /ch/"
    row (wh, w_sound)             % lesson 50 cell: "wh /w/, ph /f/"
    row (ph, f_sound)             % lesson 50 cell: "wh /w/, ph /f/"
    row (ng, ng_sound)            % lesson 51 cell: "ng /ŋ/"
    row (nk, nk_sound)            % lesson 52 cell: "nk /ŋk/"

    source "A consonant digraph is a combination of two consonant letters that represent a single consonant speech sound.  The lessons in this unit are designed to strengthen students’ familiarity with consonant digraphs."
    locator "https://ufli.education.ufl.edu/foundations/toolbox-aus/42-53/"
    trust authoritative
}
